I apologize.

Boy, I should really be careful about what I say around here.  The
kludge itself is a brilliant piece of work.  It lets thousands of
people play a wonderful game on an operating system that otherwise
wouldn't be capable of it.

What I should have said was:

"The way MS-DOS requires a kludge to get around its memory
architecture is bad enough."

Just a note here.  Roughly 1 1/2 years ago, I heard that NH3 was out.
I had just completed 2.3, and this was exciting.  So, I went over to
the friend's place, who had it.  Well, they couldn't run the whole
thing on their system.  They had to leave parts out (mainly polyself),
so that it could run on MS-DOS, even though they had 2 megs of memory.

Later, they got a version with the overlays.  It normally swapped them
in from disk.  This was really slow.  Thus, they set up a RAMdisk, and
swapped in the overlays from there.  This was still slower than it
should be, but tolerable.  Well, at this point, they had a virtual
disk, and this virtual disk was being used as virtual memory.

It suddenly occurred to me that MS-DOS has some serious problems.


